1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,819
The event loop. This is where those, what order

2
00:00:02,919 --> 00:00:05,926
does this print, questions come from. Once you see

3
00:00:06,026 --> 00:00:09,716
the three places code waits, these puzzles become mechanical.

4
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:15,232
JavaScript runs one thing at a time, on the call stack.

5
00:00:15,332 --> 00:00:18,701
Anything that has to wait goes into one of two queues.

6
00:00:18,801 --> 00:00:23,775
The macrotask queue holds timer callbacks from setTimeout and setInterval, plus

7
00:00:23,875 --> 00:00:28,721
events. The microtask queue holds promise reactions, like dot then callbacks,

8
00:00:28,821 --> 00:00:31,548
and the code that runs after fetch resolves.

9
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:35,484
And here's the rule that decides every puzzle. First, the

10
00:00:35,584 --> 00:00:39,782
whole script runs. Then every microtask runs, until that queue

11
00:00:39,882 --> 00:00:43,456
is empty. Only then does the loop take one macrotask.

12
00:00:43,556 --> 00:00:46,437
After it, all microtasks again, and repeat.

13
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:52,084
Puzzle one. On the left is the code, in the middle the call

14
00:00:52,184 --> 00:00:55,921
stack and the two queues, and on the bottom left, the console.

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,478
The script goes on the call stack and runs

16
00:00:58,578 --> 00:01:01,180
top to bottom. Line one logs Dog right away.

17
00:01:01,266 --> 00:01:05,866
Line two is setTimeout with zero milliseconds. It doesn't run the callback.

18
00:01:05,966 --> 00:01:10,378
It only registers it, and after zero milliseconds the callback goes into

19
00:01:10,478 --> 00:01:13,574
the macrotask queue, where it has to wait its turn.

20
00:01:13,666 --> 00:01:16,886
The script keeps going, and line three logs Lion. Now

21
00:01:16,986 --> 00:01:19,830
the script is finished, and the stack is empty.

22
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:22,653
There are no microtasks, so the loop takes

23
00:01:22,753 --> 00:01:25,440
the next macrotask, and finally logs Cat.

24
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:29,060
So the output is Dog, Lion, Cat. A zero millisecond

25
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:33,257
timeout still waits for all the synchronous code to finish.

26
00:01:37,333 --> 00:01:40,137
Puzzle two mixes a promise with setTimeout.

27
00:01:40,233 --> 00:01:43,878
Now let's mix in a promise. One is logged immediately. The

28
00:01:43,978 --> 00:01:48,269
timeout's callback goes to the macrotask queue, exactly like before.

29
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:51,686
Promise dot resolve gives an already resolved promise, so

30
00:01:51,786 --> 00:01:55,106
its then callback goes straight into the microtask queue.

31
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:57,836
Then four is logged, and the script is done.

32
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:00,948
Now the key rule. All microtasks run before the

33
00:02:01,048 --> 00:02:04,395
next macrotask. So three prints next, and only after

34
00:02:04,495 --> 00:02:07,577
that does the timer's callback get to print two.

35
00:02:07,666 --> 00:02:10,907
One, four, three, two. Synchronous code

36
00:02:11,007 --> 00:02:14,334
first, then microtasks, then macrotasks.

37
00:02:18,433 --> 00:02:22,205
Here's a trap the exam has used. getCount declares count, and

38
00:02:22,305 --> 00:02:25,125
sets up an interval that will set count to 10.

39
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:28,106
But the return happens right now, during the script.

40
00:02:28,206 --> 00:02:30,996
The interval callback can't run until the stack is

41
00:02:31,096 --> 00:02:34,292
empty, which is long after getCount has already returned.

42
00:02:34,666 --> 00:02:37,521
So it prints undefined. The fix is to return a

43
00:02:37,621 --> 00:02:40,926
promise and call resolve with 10 inside the callback.

44
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,334
Exam trap. A function can't return a value that only

45
00:02:45,434 --> 00:02:49,628
gets assigned later, inside a setTimeout or setInterval callback.

46
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:57,294
setInterval runs its callback again and again, every given number

47
00:02:57,394 --> 00:03:00,616
of milliseconds. It returns an id, so save it.

48
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:03,336
Each time, we bump n and log it.

49
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:07,250
When n hits 3, clearInterval with that id stops it.

50
00:03:07,350 --> 00:03:12,549
setTimeout, by contrast, runs only once, and clearTimeout cancels it.

51
00:03:12,933 --> 00:03:18,113
So we get 1, 2, and 3, one second apart, and then it stops.

52
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:23,806
Practice question 1. What does this print, in

53
00:03:23,906 --> 00:03:25,852
order? Pause the video and try it.

54
00:03:34,133 --> 00:03:38,419
E, B, D, A, C. E is the only synchronous log, so it's

55
00:03:38,519 --> 00:03:44,130
first. Then the microtasks, in the order they were queued: B, then D.

56
00:03:44,230 --> 00:03:49,593
While B ran, it queued another timer, C. Now the macrotasks run in

57
00:03:49,693 --> 00:03:54,145
order: A was queued by the script, so it goes before C.

58
00:03:58,233 --> 00:04:02,411
Practice question 2. Fix getCount so the caller can actually get the 10,

59
00:04:02,511 --> 00:04:05,501
and show how to call it. Pause the video and try it.

60
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:18,066
Return a new Promise. Inside the interval callback, clear the interval

61
00:04:18,166 --> 00:04:21,434
and call resolve with 10. The caller can't get a plain

62
00:04:21,534 --> 00:04:24,740
value back, so it uses dot then to receive the count.

63
00:04:28,833 --> 00:04:33,357
Recap. All the synchronous code runs first. Then all the microtasks,

64
00:04:33,457 --> 00:04:38,321
meaning promise callbacks. Then one macrotask, like a timer callback, and

65
00:04:38,421 --> 00:04:42,537
then microtasks again. And a value set inside a timer callback

66
00:04:42,637 --> 00:04:45,869
can't be returned directly. Wrap it in a promise.

